WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.170 --> 00:00:04.210
How, exactly, do you teach a robot?

00:00:04.210 --> 00:00:06.880
Traditionally, to get a robot to do what you want

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:08.320
requires programming--

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:10.720
a list of tasks or rules to follow--

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:12.300
which often means nuance

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.940
and the unpredictable are left out.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:17.820
Or, in many AI systems, the robot learns from

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:20.280
massive sets of training data.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:23.300
What if you could make a robot figure it out for itself,

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:26.120
based on concepts and inference?

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:28.060
Researchers have developed a new system

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:30.800
for teaching robots concepts by presenting them

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:33.280
a pair of images and having them figure out

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:37.280
how to transform one to the other in real life.

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:39.100
The goal is to introduce a method of learning

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:42.720
used by humans all the time--learning by inference—

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:44.120
like looking at the instructions

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:46.620
for assembling an IKEA chair.

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:48.680
Let’s take an easier example,

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:51.880
these two images of apples - the input, on the left,

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:54.140
and the output on the right.

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:56.040
For us, it’s easy to see how the apples

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:59.080
are reorganized by position and color.

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:01.320
The trick here is to make a robot recognize

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:04.500
the conceptual differences, plan the changes needed,

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.220
and then act on that plan.

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:09.680
Researchers experimented with two different robots,

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:12.700
each equipped with a camera at the end of a gripper

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:16.340
for manipulating different objects on a tabletop.

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:18.600
They presented the robots with a pair of images -

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:20.580
an input and an output -

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:22.700
representing a concept.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:24.720
Each concept consisted of different shapes,

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:26.920
colors, and positions, which are captured

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:30.440
by the camera and then run through special software.

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:33.140
It was then up to the robots to infer exactly how

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:35.440
to rearrange the objects on the tabletop

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:38.740
to match the output image in each concept.

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:40.580
The robots achieved that by evaluating

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:42.780
the different matchups in their imagination

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:45.799
to arrive at a program that described the concept

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:49.200
Researchers used a dataset of 546 different concepts

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:51.130
to test the robots, and the robots

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:54.380
successfully learned 535 of them.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:56.520
Researchers then tested 6 of these concepts

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.360
with real-world variations in objects, backgrounds,

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:00.440
and complexities.

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:02.860
despite those variations, the robots were able to

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:05.200
successfully execute the concepts

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:06.880
They even found that they could teach the robots

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:08.720
the steps needed by directing their

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:10.740
attention with a pointer.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:12.700
Researchers believe that in the future,

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:14.300
systems like these could be used to

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.100
help robots achieve tasks

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:18.100
without copying the movements of people

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:19.540
and in new situations

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:21.140
never encountered before...

